# Protection for Whom?
Tamil title: யாருக்குப் பாதுகாப்பு?

"I trust that you will not object to the committees being dissolved in accordance with the convention that a uniform system should be followed everywhere."

Governor's adviser Comrade T. Austin made this observation on 7 February 1944 while replying to an address of welcome presented by the Kumbakonam Devasthanam Committee.

It appears that the committee presented the address to Comrade Austin as a sign of its objection to the government's decision to dissolve the existing Devasthanam committees and establish advisory committees in their place.

We do not enter into the debate over which of the two is better: the existing committee or the advisory committee that is to be established.

We are not concerned with whatever methods may be adopted to safeguard property received in the name of religious endowments. What must be considered here is how and for what purpose that property is spent, and for what purpose it ought to be spent.

Some may think: "What is there to consider? Property received for a temple belongs to the temple and must be spent on the temple." But they forget to consider how far that belief is true.

A person earns money through various forms of work with the object of conducting his life. If anything remains after meeting those needs, he spends the surplus in other fields. No one calls such expenditure wrong. It is considered wrong only if he spends it in improper fields. No one criticises people who devote what remains after their living expenses to the public good.

Likewise, after money received for a temple has met the temple's expenses, why should the law governing religious endowments not permit the surplus to be spent in other fields serving the public good? That is our question.

Whether it is the existing Devasthanam committees or the advisory committees yet to come, our appeal is that they should undertake to spend temple revenue in fields concerned with public welfare.

When the government spends money obtained from other fields upon many temples that possess no revenue, there is no reason it should feel compelled to hesitate before spending the property of temples with high incomes on public works beneficial to the people.

Hundreds of thousands of heaps of gold have been accumulated in the name of temples, enclosed within the fortress called orthodoxy, surrounded by the wall called religion, fitted with doors called sin and merit, and locked with the lock called God. Through the cunning of deceivers, all the wealth earned by poor and humble people through their sweat and labour ends up inside this orthodox fortress. The present quarrel of "you or me?" concerns only who will guard the wealth accumulated in this way.

If the honourable gentlemen who fight to protect the money donated by poor people who have scorched in the sun, soaked in the rain, been battered by the wind, rolled in the soil, split mountains, and broken stones would reflect upon the ruined lives of the working people who are the root cause of both their comfortable existence and temple revenue, they would not have become alarmed that dissolving the Devasthanam committees would ruin the administration of religious institutions.

Does preserving the administration of religious institutions remove the suffering of the poor?

Hundreds of thousands of poor people, labouring in fear of their employers' cruelty and exhausted by excessive work, suffer from disease and poverty. Should not religious-endowment protection funds be used to cure the diseases and remove the poverty of these living images?

Does not religious scripture itself proclaim the truth that the living image, the human being, is higher than the unmoving image, the deity?

It appears that the Mayor of Madras is attempting to establish a health-protection institution, a tuberculosis hospital, for people suffering from tuberculosis and other cruel diseases caused by excessive labour. Our appeal is that temple-endowment protection funds should be used for efforts of this kind.

Until funds for protecting religion become funds for protecting people, our country will not progress, whatever efforts it undertakes.

If the various guardians of endowments unite and strive to create conditions in which the people's labour benefits the people themselves, there will be no room for endowments of this kind, money for them, or committees to protect them.

Nor will there be any need to fight the government over this.

If the people's wealth is not sucked into endowments of this kind, their lives will shine as lives of health freed from disease and poverty, elevated lives rich in knowledge, ability, and courage.

Events from the beginning of religious protection until today have firmly demonstrated that, as protection of religion grows, protection of the people steadily declines.

Can anyone point to even one benefit the people have received from protecting religion? We can demonstrate with evidence thousands of harms it has caused them.

That is why we insist that protection rightly belongs and is needed not for religion but for the people. Such endowment-protection funds must be used in fields that secure freedom from disease, removal of poverty, and growth of knowledge for the people. That is true protection.

We ask the guardians of religious institutions to understand that improving religious institutions can never improve the institutions of people's lives.

*Dravida Nadu, 13 February 1944*

---NOTES---
The title is split across columns in the first scan and reads யாருக்குப் பாதுகாப்பு?. The closing OCR date is damaged as 73..2.44; the scan sequence and article's 7 February reference support 13 February 1944 provisionally. The source's படமாடி/நடமாடி contrast is translated as unmoving divine image versus living human image.
